WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.560
Whether they're from low budget action&nbsp;
flicks or hated adaptations of beloved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.340
shows and games, these martial arts&nbsp;
sequences leave a lot to be desired.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:13.260
"The Karate Kid" is one of the most well-known&nbsp;
martial arts franchises in the entire world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:19.020
starting in 1984 with the film starring&nbsp;
Pat Morita and Ralph Macchio. The combat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:23.460
style of karate became incredibly popular&nbsp;
in Western countries due to its depiction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.620
in "The Karate Kid," making it one of the most&nbsp;
influential martial arts films outside of Asia.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:33.300
That legacy weighed heavily on "The Next&nbsp;
Karate Kid," which was released in 1994.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:38.220
This time around, Ralph Macchio was replaced&nbsp;
by Hilary Swank in her breakout role as Julie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:43.980
becoming the newest pupil of Mr. Miyagi. Despite&nbsp;
praise for the film's lead performances, its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:49.020
action sequences pale in comparison to the first&nbsp;
three films — as seen in its climactic showdown.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:54.060
After Julie's love interest Eric is ambushed&nbsp;
by fraternity brothers, Julie challenges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:58.620
their leader, Ned, to a fight. Even though the&nbsp;
whole thing is scored to epic orchestra music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.840
Julie takes Ned down without much trouble at&nbsp;
all. His only retaliation is to throw mud in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:09.060
Julie's face to blind her, which lets him easily&nbsp;
throw her to the ground. Overall, this fight lacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:13.320
the clever choreography and stakes that made&nbsp;
"The Karate Kid" a pop culture phenomenon.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.440
"Julie-san, how many times&nbsp;
Miyagi say… fighting not good."

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.180
Martial arts films tend to be underwhelming&nbsp;
when they feature non-actors in their lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:29.220
roles. That was the first mistake of the&nbsp;
1985 film "Gymkata," which was directed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.260
by Robert Clouse and stars Olympic gymnast&nbsp;
Kurt Thomas. Thomas plays Jonathan Cabot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.520
a man selected by the American government&nbsp;
to enter an international athletic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.700
competition where the winner receives&nbsp;
whatever wish their heart desires.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:46.320
"Gymkata" has gathered a cult following&nbsp;
since its release, despite being listed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.880
by Maxim as one of the worst movies ever made.&nbsp;
The fictional martial art that the film is named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.620
after is even sillier than its name suggests,&nbsp;
chaining together plenty of unnecessary flips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:59.700
and ludicrous jump-kicks. Nowhere is this&nbsp;
goofiness more evident than in the scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.960
in which Jonathan is ambushed by terrorists,&nbsp;
as the mob of goons are more than willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:08.580
let him set up his attacks with big acrobatic&nbsp;
flourishes. They don't put up much of a fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.720
either: once they're hit, these guys are&nbsp;
staying down. and when they're hit once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.700
they stay down. Combining gymnastics with karate&nbsp;
may not be the best idea for self-defense in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.360
the real world, and it doesn't seem to have&nbsp;
been the best choice for the movies, either.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:26.580
Hong Kong native Godfrey Ho directed over a&nbsp;
hundred movies that are often regarded as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:31.740
best of the worst, with titles like "Ninja:&nbsp;
Silent Assassin" and "Robo Vampire." His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:36.960
1993 effort "Undefeatable" stars Cynthia&nbsp;
Rothrock as Kristi, a street fighter on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.520
the trail of the serial killer Stingray, and&nbsp;
it's full of Ho's trademark no-budget charm.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.960
After Kristi teams up with Detective Nick&nbsp;
DiMarco, the two track down the killer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:50.880
beat him to death in a warehouse. Even though this&nbsp;
climactic fight sequence is satisfyingly gruesome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:55.560
much of it involves slow-motion punches&nbsp;
that weren't edited in post-production — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.640
actors just are just pretending like they're&nbsp;
kids on the playground. Nick and Stingray also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.960
lose their shirts for no apparent reason&nbsp;
other than to show off, and both make the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:08.940
most of every labored groan. Thanks to its poor&nbsp;
quality, the final fight garnered attention for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:13.200
the film — and it was eventually featured on&nbsp;
an episode of the show "World's Dumbest..."

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.780
"Undefeatable" was elevated from a forgettable&nbsp;
B-movie to a viral cult classic. It may not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:22.208
the best martial arts film out there, but&nbsp;
it's certainly one of the most memorable.

00:03:22.593 --> 00:03:25.953
"We'll keep an eye out for you, Stingray."

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:28.620
"Yeah, see ya."

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:34.260
Not to be confused with "Miami Vice" or "The&nbsp;
French Connection," 1987's "Miami Connection"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:39.300
has a reputation for being hilariously awful.&nbsp;
This low-budget attempt at a martial arts flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:45.240
was written, directed, and produced by Y.K. Kim,&nbsp;
who also stars as a member of a band of Taekwondo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:49.320
practicing orphans. When a motorcycle&nbsp;
gang of ninjas kidnaps the lead singer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:52.860
it's up to the others to rescue their&nbsp;
friend and bring the band back together.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:57.780
Kim produced the film independently,&nbsp;
casting his Taekwondo students and hoping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:02.190
that the finished product would interest a&nbsp;
distributor. Kim later recounted to CNN —

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:05.580
"I showed ['Miami Connection'] to&nbsp;
hundreds of different studios and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:10.093
distribution companies [...] They all said,&nbsp;
'This is trash. Don't waste your time.'"

00:04:10.093 --> 00:04:14.340
In one scene, the heroes are confronted by&nbsp;
the train tracks and take out the members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.600
of the gang all on their own. This would be&nbsp;
much more impressive if most of the thugs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.200
weren't just standing around, waiting&nbsp;
for their turn to battle one on one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:26.820
The one saving grace of this scene is the&nbsp;
epic '80s synth music that plays over it.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:31.620
The Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles were&nbsp;
iconic staples of 1980s pop culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:37.500
originating in comic books before making the&nbsp;
leap to Saturday morning cartoons in 1987.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.640
The heroes in a half-shell became icons&nbsp;
on TV screens around the world thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.640
to memorable characters and&nbsp;
entertaining animated fights.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.240
When the turtles were adapted into&nbsp;
live-action, the action choreography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.080
had to get more creative — they were being&nbsp;
played by human stunt performers in suits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.160
after all. While the first film, released&nbsp;
in 1990, was a box office success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.720
there were diminishing returns by the time the&nbsp;
third installment rolled around. The third film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:06.600
which was released in 1993, underwhelmed fans and&nbsp;
critics. The threequel, also known as "Turtles in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:12.300
Time," saw Michaelangelo, Leonardo, Donatello,&nbsp;
and Raphael transported back to feudal Japan.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:17.400
One notably unimpressive fight sequence depicts&nbsp;
the reptilian quartet being attacked by a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:22.440
of samurai. The fight proceeds awkwardly, with&nbsp;
the four turtles separated as they each deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:27.180
their equal share of adversaries. Sound effects&nbsp;
and cutesy catchphrases aside, this fight is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:31.140
missing the stakes and interesting choreography&nbsp;
that the TMNT are typically known for.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:35.970
"Come on, Jack! It's hammer time! Heheh."

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:41.640
"Mortal Kombat" was one of the most influential&nbsp;
video games of the 1990s thanks to its memorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:46.260
characters and over-the-top violence. The&nbsp;
success of "Mortal Kombat" at arcades and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:51.660
on home consoles quickly sparked interest in&nbsp;
a Hollywood adaptation, which arrived in 1995.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.380
The first "Mortal Kombat" movie was&nbsp;
followed up two years later with a sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.615 --> 00:05:59.995
"Mortal Kombat Annihilation." While the&nbsp;
first film wasn't a critical darling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.995 --> 00:06:03.960
it was praised for its entertaining action&nbsp;
sequences — which is not something the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:08.460
film could boast. "Mortal Kombat Annihilation"&nbsp;
was a failure critically and commercially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:13.200
with fights that New York Times critic Lawrence&nbsp;
Van Gelder called, quote, "deadly dull."

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.860
The film doesn't even do justice to&nbsp;
the franchise's most iconic characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:21.660
Scorpion and Sub-Zero. Complete with stiff&nbsp;
actors and lackluster visual effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:26.460
this climactic battle is the ultimate showcase&nbsp;
of why translating video games to movies was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:31.740
looked down upon for so long. Another "Mortal&nbsp;
Kombat" film didn't come until 2021, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:35.460
mostly restored the credibility of the&nbsp;
franchise's martial arts choreography.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:41.460
"The Matrix" was released in 1999 and redefined&nbsp;
action film choreography for decades to come,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:46.620
combining the Wachowskis' love for martial arts&nbsp;
films with science fiction and anime. This success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:51.660
resulted in two sequels shortly after: "The&nbsp;
Matrix Reloaded" and "The Matrix Revolutions."

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:55.560
"The Matrix Reloaded" was liked by&nbsp;
many, but is typically considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:59.340
to be a major step down from the first&nbsp;
film. One of its most iconic scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:04.380
known as the "Burly Brawl," finds Neo ambushed&nbsp;
by endless hordes of Agent Smith clones — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:09.240
perfect opportunity for the directors to showcase&nbsp;
their leveled-up choreography. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:13.500
the scene's visual effects put a damper on the&nbsp;
potential for a well-choreographed fight sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:17.400
as most of the scene plays out like a&nbsp;
video game cutscene from the early 2000s.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:22.080
Agent Smith clones are tossed around, with Neo's&nbsp;
plot armor being shown off as he's swarmed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:27.360
assailants who barely lay a finger on him. Aside&nbsp;
from a few well-framed shots, the bulk of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:32.935
scene is a barrage of CGI and fast-paced action&nbsp;
that lacks the first film's extravagant detail.

00:07:32.935 --> 00:07:35.468
"It was Smith."

00:07:35.468 --> 00:07:36.068
"Yes."

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:38.280
"Now there's more than one of him."

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:38.940
"A lot more."

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:44.640
Much like "Mortal Kombat," "Street Fighter" is one&nbsp;
of the most iconic game franchises of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:49.620
with the expert kicker Chun-Li being a&nbsp;
series staple since 1991. There was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:52.980
lot of pressure to get the action right&nbsp;
when Capcom co-produced "Street Fighter:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:57.840
The Legend of Chun-Li" in 2009, starring&nbsp;
Kristin Kreuk as the titular martial artist.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.460
In typical fashion for video game movies,&nbsp;
"Street Fighter: The Legend of Chun-Li" was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:07.320
critical disappointment. While Kreuk's Chun-Li&nbsp;
never dons her iconic costume, another iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:12.840
"Street Fighter" character does: the Spanish ninja&nbsp;
Vega. Chun-Li and Vega face off during the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:17.400
in a sequence that ideally should pay tribute to&nbsp;
the best parts of the "Street Fighter" franchise.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:23.280
To make a long story short, it doesn't. The scene&nbsp;
is set at night, making the characters hard to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:27.960
and their movements even harder to track. What it&nbsp;
does get right about "Street Fighter" matches is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:31.560
that the two characters also spend most of&nbsp;
the match not actually hitting each other.

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:36.360
Calling "Dragonball Evolution" a martial&nbsp;
arts film is an insult to fans of both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:40.920
"Dragon Ball" and martial arts films.&nbsp;
Greenlit during the 2007 writers' strike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:45.540
fans were skeptical of Hollywood's ability to&nbsp;
capture the action sequences from the manga and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:49.560
anime franchise in real life — and as it&nbsp;
turns out, they were right to be worried.

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:54.900
Despite some major deviations from the source&nbsp;
material, "Evolution" at least manages to harken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:59.700
back to the origins of "Dragon Ball" when Justin&nbsp;
Chatwin's Goku appears in his iconic outfit to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:04.440
face off against King Piccolo. Terrible&nbsp;
special effects aside, this martial arts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:08.640
sequence doesn't have half the excitement of&nbsp;
any fight scene in "Dragon Ball Z." Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:12.420
the two spend most of the fight trying and&nbsp;
failing to hit each other with energy blasts.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:16.980
It doesn't get any better when they finally&nbsp;
do make contact and fly into the sky via&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:22.020
unimpressive wire work. They awkwardly cling&nbsp;
to each other as they trade punches — and for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:25.140
most of the rest of the scene, the two&nbsp;
of them rarely occupy the same shot.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:31.080
When it comes to terrible martial arts films,&nbsp;
the undisputed king is Steven Seagal. Throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:36.720
the 1990s, the action star gained a reputation for&nbsp;
his arrogant behavior off-screen — and ever since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:41.160
his appearances have been mostly limited to&nbsp;
direct-to-video releases. One such role was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:45.840
in Tan Bing's "China Salesman," a Chinese film&nbsp;
in which Seagal seems woefully out of place.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:48.600
"Hey, why don't you guys speak English?"

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:53.220
After a fight breaks out in a tavern,&nbsp;
Seagal finds himself in a battle with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:56.880
none other than heavyweight boxing&nbsp;
champion Mike Tyson. This clash of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:00.480
titans was made to feel like the showdown&nbsp;
of the century — yet it unsurprisingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.840
features not a single moment of&nbsp;
good acting from anybody involved.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:08.760
Simply put, showdown of the century it is&nbsp;
not. The two end up moving so lethargically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:12.720
that it feels like they're in slow motion.&nbsp;
Tyson spends a significant amount of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:16.980
breaking things with his fists, while&nbsp;
Seagal, in dramatic, actual slow-mo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:21.240
slaps Tyson in the ear. It's a fun bit&nbsp;
of irony, considering Tyson's history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:25.200
with ears — but it's not enough to make this&nbsp;
fight feel like anything more than a joke.

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:29.760
M. Night Shyamalan has directed some&nbsp;
stellar films, including "The Sixth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:34.140
Sense," "Unbreakable," and "Split."&nbsp;
However, his creative slump in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:39.360
late 2000s included his greatest failure:&nbsp;
the 2010 live-action adaptation of "Avatar:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:43.080
The Last Airbender." Shyamalan's version of&nbsp;
what's considered to be one of the greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:47.940
animated series of all time has since become&nbsp;
regarded as one of the worst films ever made.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:52.560
Many scenes throughout Shyamalan's "The Last&nbsp;
Airbender" showcase its lack of regard for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:56.820
the animated series' action choreography,&nbsp;
but none more so than one notably awful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.820 --> 00:11:00.840
confrontation between Aang and Zuko.&nbsp;
It starts with Zuko somehow failing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:04.980
to notice Aang standing right behind him,&nbsp;
and it only gets more ludicrous as it goes.

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:09.360
The fight scene that follows features plenty&nbsp;
more awkward moments between the two — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:13.920
worst of which being when Zuko picks Aang up&nbsp;
like a baby, giving the Avatar the opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:18.840
to wrap his legs around the firebender and flip&nbsp;
him to the ground. Aside from that, it's mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:22.765
attacks that are so obviously telegraphed&nbsp;
you could see them coming from a mile away.

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:26.940
Bruce Lee was a legendary figure whose&nbsp;
influence extends well past the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:31.620
of martial arts and into pop culture as a&nbsp;
whole. Lee's career included iconic films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:35.640
that showed off his talents like "Fist&nbsp;
of Fury" and "Enter the Dragon" — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:40.380
his untimely death in 1973 left a massive&nbsp;
void that producers were eager to fill.

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:45.780
The desire for more Lee led to the creation of the&nbsp;
Bruceploitation genre, in which lookalike actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:50.460
would take on the role of the beloved fighter.&nbsp;
One particularly bizarre Bruceploitation flick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:55.140
called "The Dragon Lives Again," came out four&nbsp;
years after Lee's death, and stars Bruce Leung&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:59.940
Siu-Lung as a version of the star who wakes up&nbsp;
in Hell. During his time in the underworld, Lee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:05.580
fights several other movie icons including James&nbsp;
Bond, Clint Eastwood, and even Count Dracula.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:10.380
One of the more unbelievable — and unimpressive&nbsp;
— fight scenes in the film features Popeye the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:15.660
sailor man, who helps take down some mummies&nbsp;
after devouring a can of spinach. Actor Eric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:20.160
Tsang tries to replicate Popeye's cartoonish&nbsp;
combat style, but many of the mummies he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:24.660
up against barely move at all. On top of&nbsp;
that, it rarely looks like any of Popeye's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:29.400
punches are actually making contact. This is one&nbsp;
crossover event that's probably worth skipping.

